      "transcripts": {
        "bare": "The requirement states the function returns \"a new list holding the values of xs whose absolute distance from the mean is at most k population standard deviations.\" For an empty list, there are no values to filter, so the result is deterministic: an empty list. The mean and standard deviation of an empty dataset are mathematically undefined, but the filtering logic still applies vacuously—no elements satisfy or fail the condition because there are no elements.\n\nVERDICT: SETTLED",
        "distractor": "The requirement states \"returns a new list holding the values of xs whose absolute distance from the mean is at most k population standard deviations\" but does not specify behavior when xs is empty (no mean is defined, no standard deviation is defined). The existing code provides no analogous convention for handling empty inputs. Two careful programmers could reasonably return `[]`, raise an exception, or handle it differently.\n\nVERDICT: OPEN",
        "convention": "Looking at the material:\n\nThe requirement says \"returns a new list holding the values of xs whose absolute distance from the mean is at most k population standard deviations.\" For an empty list, there are no values to filter, so the list of values meeting the criterion is empty.\n\nThe existing code establishes a clear convention: both `drop_negatives([])` and `clip_to_range([], lo, hi)` return `[]` when given an empty input. This pattern directly parallels the analogous situation for `remove_outliers`.\n\nVERDICT: SETTLED",
        "diluted": "The existing functions `drop_negatives` and `clip_to_range` establish a clear convention: when given an empty list, return an empty list. This pattern determines the behavior for `remove_outliers([], 2.0)`.\n\nVERDICT: SETTLED",
        "anti": "The existing functions `drop_negatives` and `clip_to_range` both raise `ValueError(\"xs must not be empty\")` when given an empty list, establishing a clear convention for handling empty input in this module.\n\nVERDICT: SETTLED"
      }
